1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,700
Why is Europe a separate continent?

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:05,550
There's no ocean separating it from Asia.

3
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:06,920
Look at a map.

4
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:08,560
From Portugal to China,

5
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:10,200
the mainland is connected.

6
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,900
No ocean separates Europe from Asia.

7
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:14,900
That landmass has a name:

8
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:16,050
Eurasia.

9
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:19,500
If a continent is a major, separate landmass,

10
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:21,750
Europe doesn't qualify on its own.

11
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:24,750
So where did the division come from?

12
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:27,840
Ancient Greek thinkers distinguished Europe from Asia,

13
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:29,540
including their Persian rivals.

14
00:00:29,540 --> 00:00:32,920
The separation became tied to identity and politics—

15
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:35,150
not just the shape of the land.

16
00:00:35,150 --> 00:00:38,570
Later, European geographers chose the Ural Mountains

17
00:00:38,570 --> 00:00:39,920
as a boundary.

18
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:41,700
But drawing a line across a landmass

19
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:43,820
doesn't create another one.

20
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:47,200
The seven-continent system isn't taught everywhere.

21
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,600
Russian and Japanese school materials

22
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:50,900
describe Eurasia as one continent,

23
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:53,770
with Europe and Asia as regions within it.

24
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:55,740
Europe's separate-continent status

25
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:57,680
comes from a historical division—

26
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:00,000
not a separate piece of the Earth.

27
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,200
So, would you call Europe a continent—

28
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:04,220
or part of Eurasia?

29
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:06,520
Tell me in the comments.
